#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028 :topic/difficulty 0.8139321757155085 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428422 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1867->3029. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "26e7d665-1fe7-5eaf-8ec8-486cb95ff36f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119661 :question/id #uuid "7cce27e9-e2b2-599c-9f4e-437caf1a23ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-5415" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n7-3=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be3d5a74-259e-58b9-948d-47ba7412ab08" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Rank of a Matrix Given the Number of Pivot Columns"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119662 :question/id #uuid "a6a3db41-6033-537a-bc9d-03fde02dc869" :question/math-academy-id "e-5417" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4?$\n\n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows. \n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\textrm{rank}(C)=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d847ec9-c379-5013-ab77-a9b92dc8ca27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix With a Given Rank by Inspection"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119663 :question/id #uuid "b3e4eb01-0669-586b-a716-aef87d1c3580" :question/math-academy-id "e-5418" :question/problem "If \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n, find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5a44c9-16e5-55fb-a10f-15435a0a9a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank of a Matrix Using Row Reduction"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1867 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :topic/title "The Rank of a Matrix"}]}
